= ψ Filter =

{{Plain_Hook
| in_one_sentence    = A ψ filter exploits the frequency dependence of the dispersion relation — through either the waveguide cutoff mechanism (high-pass) or the resonator bandwidth mechanism (bandpass) — to selectively transmit or block specific ψ frequency ranges, enabling the [[Psi Defender]] to reject psionic interference and the [[Psi Harmonizer]] to select the operator's coherence frequency for group amplification.
| everyday_analogy   = A coffee filter passes liquid but blocks grounds — it filters by particle size. An audio equaliser filters by frequency, boosting or cutting specific ranges. A ψ filter is an audio equaliser for the psionic field: it passes some ψ frequencies while blocking others, with the passband and stopband determined by the geometry (channel width sets the cutoff frequency) and the resonator Q (Q sets the bandpass bandwidth).
| what_it_predicts   = (1) A sharp high-pass cutoff at <math>\omega_c</math> set by the channel geometry — below this frequency, ψ is completely blocked; (2) A resonant bandpass response with bandwidth inversely proportional to Q_ψ — high-Q gives narrow bandwidth and high frequency selectivity; (3) A stopband attenuation that increases with the number of cascaded filter sections; (4) A filter response tunable via BST bias voltage (shifting resonant frequency) and ferrofluid H-field (shifting cutoff frequency).
| if_you_remember_one_thing = The ψ filter is not a separate device — it is any ψ waveguide (which acts as a high-pass filter) or any ψ resonator (which acts as a bandpass filter) with appropriate geometry. Every ψ waveguide already filters; the question is only whether the filtering is intentional and engineered to a specification.
}}

== Mathematical Origin ==

=== High-Pass Filter: Waveguide Cutoff ===

From [[ψ Waveguide]] (C2), the axial wave vector of the ψ₁₀ guided mode:

:<math>k_z = \sqrt{\omega^2 - (\pi/a)^2 - m_{\rm eff}^2}</math>

For <math>\omega < \omega_c = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^2 + (\pi/a)^2}</math>: <math>k_z = i\kappa</math> (imaginary), producing evanescent decay:

:<math>\psi(z) = \psi(0)\,e^{-\kappa z}, \qquad \kappa = \sqrt{\omega_c^2 - \omega^2}</math>

Stopband attenuation per unit length:

:<math>\alpha_{\rm stop} = \kappa = \sqrt{\omega_c^2 - \omega^2} \quad\text{(nepers/m, natural units)}</math>

In dB per metre: <math>A_{\rm dB/m} = 20\log_{10}(e) \times \kappa \approx 8.686\,\kappa</math>

At <math>\omega = 0.5\omega_c</math>: <math>\kappa = \omega_c\sqrt{1-0.25} = 0.866\omega_c</math>. For <math>\omega_c/(2\pi) = 16</math> kHz (1 cm channel): <math>\kappa \approx 87</math> m⁻¹ in natural units, giving <math>A \approx 755</math> dB/m. In practice, a few cm of channel length in the stopband provides complete ψ attenuation.

=== Bandpass Filter: Resonator ===

A [[ψ Resonator]] (C1) acts as a bandpass filter. Its transmission response (from input coupling port to output coupling port):

:<math>|T(\omega)|^2 = \frac{1}{1 + Q_L^2\left(\frac{\omega}{\omega_0} - \frac{\omega_0}{\omega}\right)^2}</math>

This is a Lorentzian (second-order) bandpass response. The −3 dB bandwidth:

:<math>\Delta\omega = \frac{\omega_0}{Q_L}</math>

At Q_L = 200, <math>\omega_0/(2\pi) = 40</math> kHz: <math>\Delta f = 200</math> Hz bandwidth. This is an extremely narrow filter — it would pass only the 200 Hz band centred on 40 kHz and reject everything else.

For the [[Psi Harmonizer]] group session: each operator's coherence frequency is slightly different (varying with individual physiology). The bandpass filter must be tunable to track each operator's frequency. BST bias voltage provides ±5% frequency tuning (from [[ψ Resonator]] C1), sufficient to accommodate natural operator-to-operator frequency variation.

=== Multipole Bandpass Filter ===

Cascading N resonator sections produces an N-pole bandpass filter with steeper roll-off:

:<math>|T_N(\omega)|^2 \approx \frac{1}{1 + \left(\frac{\omega - \omega_0}{\Delta\omega/2}\right)^{2N}}</math>

Roll-off rate: <math>20N</math> dB/decade beyond the band edge. A 2-pole Chebyshev filter provides 40 dB/decade roll-off with 0.5 dB passband ripple — adequate for [[Psi Harmonizer]] frequency selection.

