== Psi Defender Filter Specification ==

The [[Psi Defender]] requires rejection of psionic perturbation across a broad frequency range (10 Hz to 10 kHz estimated from [[Psionic Warfare]] operational parameters) while passing the operator's own coherence frequency (40 kHz Stabilizer operating band). The combined filter:

# '''Bandpass at 40 kHz:''' Single resonator section passing the operator's Stabilizer frequency; Q_L ≈ 50 (deliberately low for broad acceptance band). Bandwidth: 800 Hz centred on 40 kHz.
# '''High-pass at 5 kHz:''' TPMS channel with cutoff at 5 kHz (channel width <math>a = 3</math> cm). Rejects all perturbation below 5 kHz.
# '''Evanescent sections:''' 2 cm sections of below-cutoff TPMS between the filter stages; > 100 dB isolation.

Combined: passes 39.6–40.4 kHz; rejects < 5 kHz by > 100 dB; rejects 5–39.6 kHz and 40.4+ kHz by the bandpass roll-off.

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

Within the Track B framework, the operator's biological coherence frequency is not exactly 40 kHz — it is an effective psionic frequency determined by the operator's sustained background field ψ₀ and the nonlinear effective mass:

:<math>\omega_{\rm bio}^{\rm eff} = \sqrt{m_{\rm eff}^{\rm (bio)}(\psi_0)^2 + k_{\rm bio}^2}</math>

The filter must track this individually varying frequency. The Mk2.0 [[Anchor Controller]] (D6) tracks the operator's RMSSD-proxy estimate of ω_bio^eff and adjusts the BST bias to keep the filter passband centred on the operator's current coherence frequency. This is the biological analogue of automatic frequency control (AFC) in radio receivers.

